WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.080
While redheaded Russian spies, grumpy wizards,
and even talking raccoons have become household

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.589
names, one of the first Avengers has flown
way under the radar for far too long.

00:00:09.589 --> 00:00:15.369
From her first appearance in 1963 to her latest
incarnation, the Wasp has been setting milestones.

00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:18.920
For a character known for shrinking, she's
grown more than just about any costumed hero

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.970
in Marvel's pantheon, so here's the untold
truth of the Wasp.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:24.970
Avenger Assembler

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:29.380
It’s taken some time or the Wasp to get
her due in the MCU, but in the comics, she

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:30.970
was there from the beginning.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.480
Along with Thor, Iron Man, Hulk, and Ant-Man,
The Wasp was one of the original five members

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.550
of the team that appeared in Avengers #1 — and
she even gave the team its name.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.460
After the Hulk asks what the heroes should
call their new team, the Wasp suggests "Avengers,"

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.550
and Ant-Man approves the name before she even
gets to finish the sentence.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:53.480
With one heroic pose, the team has been christened,
and the name has stuck for Earth’s Mightiest

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.710
Heroes ever since.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.670
Janet takes the chair

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:01.750
Along with being a founding Avenger and the
one to name her home team, the Wasp is a landmark

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:05.390
Avenger in that she was the team's first female
chairperson, keeping her job through a healthy

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:06.680
chunk of the '80s.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.320
In Avengers #217, the Wasp called for elections
for a new chairperson, nominated herself,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.040
and quickly won, with the support of Captain
America, Thor, and Iron Man.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.960
She was a popular and effective leader, and
remains one of the few Marvel heroes who can

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:24.280
give orders to Captain America without making
you wonder why he'd follow them.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.360
Save for a brief leave of absence when the
Vision took over the team, the Wasp remained

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.590
chairperson for five years until Avengers
#278, when she stepped down after a brutal

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:39.060
storyline that saw the Masters of Evil successfully
invade Avengers Mansion and, among other things,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.190
beat their butler, Edwin Jarvis, so badly
that he wound up in the hospital, and put

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.920
Hercules in a coma.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.200
The Wasp that almost was

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.130
In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, the Wasp
isn’t the Janet Van Dyne that we know from

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.130
the comics.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:56.520
Instead, that codename went to Hope Van Dyne,
played by Evangeline Lily.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.560
She first appeared in 2015’s Ant-Man and
finally got her own super-powered shrinking

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.119
costume in Ant-Man and the Wasp.

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:07.400
In a 2012 Q&amp;A, however, Avengers director
Joss Whedon revealed that she almost made

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.170
her way to the big screen a few years earlier.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:14.260
Worried that Scarlett Johansson wouldn’t
return to the MCU as Black Widow, Whedon wrote

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.980
what he called “a very Wasp-y draft” that
featured the tiny heroine in a tribute to

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.180
the Wasp’s status as a founding Avenger.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:25.120
Obviously, Black Widow did stick around as
a core member of the team, but finding out

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.049
how close we got does leave us wondering:
why not have both?

00:02:29.049 --> 00:02:31.359
The Girl who was The Wasp

00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:36.079
Stieg Larsson’s novels about antisocial
hacker Lisbeth Salander were published posthumously,

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:40.879
but were popular enough that author David
Lagercrantz was tapped to continue the series.

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:45.200
In the first of Lagercrantz's additions to
the Millennium series, The Girl in the Spider's

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.730
Web, Lagercrantz reveals that was Janet Van
Dyne who inspired Lisbeth to give herself

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:54.739
the hacker ID of "Wasp," as well as inspiring
the wasp tattoo on the back of Lisbeth's neck.

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:59.190
At first glance, it would be easy to assume
that Lagercrantz is simply an old Marvel fan

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.879
who threw in his own childhood favorites,
which checks out when you discover that the

00:03:02.879 --> 00:03:07.939
book also included villains using names like
Thanos and Zemo, but the author insists this

00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:09.109
isn't the case.

00:03:09.109 --> 00:03:13.510
Speaking with The Verge in 2015, Lagercrantz
claimed he hadn't read Marvel comics as a

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:18.010
child and that he was googling the Wasp while
trying to figure out why Larsson gave Lisbeth

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:19.200
the codename.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.159
According to him, his image search turned
up a character who looked exactly like Lisbeth

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:24.439
Salander.

00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:28.209
If there's anything that would make a Marvel
fan raise an eyebrow at Lagercrantz's story,

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:31.559
it's the notion that he was able to find a
picture of the Wasp that looked anything like

00:03:31.559 --> 00:03:32.659
Lisbeth.

00:03:32.659 --> 00:03:37.999
The only times Janet Van Dyne looked even
slightly goth were alternate universes.

00:03:37.999 --> 00:03:39.999
Seven names aren’t enough

00:03:39.999 --> 00:03:44.670
2009’s Secret Invasion event concluded with,
among other things, the apparent death of

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.250
Janet van Dyne.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:50.299
Four years later, it was revealed that she’d
actually been trapped in a microscopic universe

00:03:50.299 --> 00:03:54.310
instead, because in superhero comics, that’s
something that actually makes a lot of sense.

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:59.010
In the meantime Hank Pym — Janet’s ex-husband
and on-again/off-again lover — paid tribute

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:03.419
to Janet by taking up the name of "The Wasp",
and led a new lineup of Earth's mightiest

00:04:03.419 --> 00:04:05.819
heroes in the pages of Mighty Avengers.

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:09.879
What makes that notable is that it’s actually
just one in a string of codenames Pym has

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:11.249
given himself.

00:04:11.249 --> 00:04:16.550
Over the years, he’s been known as Ant-Man,
Giant Man, Goliath, Gigantus, Yellowjacket,

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:18.780
Doctor Pym, and even Ultron.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.780
It's enough to make you wonder if the guy
was just waiting for Janet to die so he could

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.560
add one more superhero name to his collection.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.840
The Unstoppable Wasp

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.120
The most recent heir to take up the mantle
of the Wasp is Nadia van Dyne — daughter

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.970
of Hank Pym and Hungarian scientist Maria
Travoya.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:39.120
The new Wasp first appeared in a 2016 Free
Comic Book Day issue and soon was a regular

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.410
member of the All-New, All-Different Avengers.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:47.009
Nadia is an interesting fusion of her father,
her namesake, and the Black Widow.

00:04:47.009 --> 00:04:51.240
Raised in the same infamous Red Room that
made Natasha Romanoff the spy she is, Nadia

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.040
escaped only when she was able to get her
hands on the Pym particles that fueled her

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.610
father’s powers.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:00.669
After a reboot of Avengers, Nadia enjoyed
a shot at her own ongoing title, the only

00:05:00.669 --> 00:05:03.449
solo Marvel Wasp title ever.

00:05:03.449 --> 00:05:08.530
For eight issues, she starred in The Unstoppable
Wasp, and founded her own team: the Genius

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:12.970
In action Research Lab, also known as the
Agents of G.I.R.L.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:13.970
Thanks for watching!

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.770
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
